WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:09.540
Arthur C. Clarke, author of "2001: A Space
Odyssey", once said: "Two possibilities exist:

00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:14.240
Either we are alone in the
universe, or we are not. Both are equally terrifying."

00:00:14.540 --> 00:00:17.550
Most media exploring the
topic of alien life in the universe

00:00:17.550 --> 00:00:21.660
often work from the assumption that we
are not alone, and that our technology is

00:00:21.660 --> 00:00:24.960
not sufficiently advanced to detect our
distant neighbors, or that they know

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:29.220
we're here and don't think we're worth
contacting, or any number of other explanations.

00:00:29.220 --> 00:00:31.840
While these hypotheses may turn out to be true,

00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:35.250
the other possibility that
doesn't get as much attention is that we

00:00:35.250 --> 00:00:39.720
are, in fact, alone in the universe. If
that were the case, how would it change

00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:43.860
our lives and our goals here on Earth?
For this thought experiment, we're going

00:00:43.860 --> 00:00:47.040
to assume that humanity has a way to
accurately determine that we are the

00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:51.630
only life forms in the universe, and work
from there. If one day, the news broke

00:00:51.630 --> 00:00:55.590
that the universe is indeed a cold, dead
place, and that all life is contained

00:00:55.590 --> 00:01:00.000
here on Earth, it would instantly bring
to mind two possibilities: Either we are

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:04.769
the very first life in the universe, or
the very last — and knowing humanity's

00:01:04.769 --> 00:01:09.060
affinity for giant explosives, we could
very easily be the first AND last life

00:01:09.060 --> 00:01:13.259
in the universe if we weren't careful.
Besides our new terrifying realization

00:01:13.259 --> 00:01:17.009
that we are one planet away from a
completely sterile universe, what would

00:01:17.009 --> 00:01:21.719
change? Let's start with the world's
space programs. If we were alone in the

00:01:21.719 --> 00:01:24.899
universe, we could scrap any missions
designed to search for extraterrestrial

00:01:24.899 --> 00:01:29.069
life. That would include numerous probes,
such as those intended for the icy

00:01:29.069 --> 00:01:32.969
landscapes of Europa and Enceladus, as
well as entire branches of national

00:01:32.969 --> 00:01:36.329
space programs like the SETI Institute.
We could use the money from those

00:01:36.329 --> 00:01:39.479
programs to redouble our efforts in
preserving the earth and its natural

00:01:39.479 --> 00:01:43.770
resources, or to fund new schools,
hospitals, or humanitarian organizations.

00:01:43.770 --> 00:01:48.389
Of course, that's probably wishful
thinking, but the possibility is there.

00:01:48.389 --> 00:01:52.289
If we wanted to keep that money in the
space programs, we could put it towards

00:01:52.289 --> 00:01:56.039
research and development of ways to
ensure humanity's continued survival in

00:01:56.039 --> 00:02:01.259
the event of disaster on Earth. Colonies
on the Moon, Venus, Mars, or even prototype

00:02:01.259 --> 00:02:04.439
generational ships that could support
human colonies over several lifetimes

00:02:04.439 --> 00:02:08.759
would be worthwhile endeavors. Of course,
with an announcement of such magnitude,

00:02:08.760 --> 00:02:12.600
it would no doubt create polarized
groups within our global society.

00:02:12.600 --> 00:02:17.100
As the only intelligent life in the universe,
would it be our responsibility to

00:02:17.100 --> 00:02:21.330
seed life on other planets by sending probes
full of bacteria and simple organisms, or

00:02:21.330 --> 00:02:24.840
are we no more than a blight on the
universe that should be contained to Earth?

00:02:24.840 --> 00:02:28.600
Given our history of colonization
on Earth, it would stand to reason that

00:02:28.600 --> 00:02:31.820
we would at least want to establish
colonies as far away from Earth as

00:02:31.830 --> 00:02:35.550
possible, if only to ensure the survival
of our species in the event of a

00:02:35.550 --> 00:02:39.660
gamma-ray burst or some other cataclysm.
But seeding life on other planets we'll

00:02:39.660 --> 00:02:44.010
likely never visit would probably be met
with controversy. So far, we've been

00:02:44.010 --> 00:02:48.000
working under the assumption that we are
the only life in the universe at all,

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:52.140
but what if we specified that we were the
only intelligent life? How would that

00:02:52.160 --> 00:02:56.010
change the scenario? Well, if we assume
again that humanity has a way to

00:02:56.010 --> 00:02:59.720
determine that there is no intelligent
life in the universe, then not much will

00:02:59.730 --> 00:03:03.840
change initially. However, imagine a
scenario in which a probe from Earth

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:08.550
discovers single-celled life on another
planet, let's say Europa. In this scenario,

00:03:08.550 --> 00:03:12.480
we would be the billions of years more
advanced alien species keeping an eye on

00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:15.620
new life in the universe — something that
many people have suspected

00:03:15.620 --> 00:03:19.380
extraterrestrials have been doing to us
over the history of life on Earth.

00:03:19.380 --> 00:03:23.240
But what would we do about it?
To what extent would we interfere with

00:03:23.250 --> 00:03:26.790
the growth of a new present in the
universe? Many would argue that the best

00:03:26.790 --> 00:03:30.720
course of action would be to observe
from afar, and allow nature to run its

00:03:30.720 --> 00:03:34.500
course no matter the outcome. Others
might want to watch, but take it one step

00:03:34.500 --> 00:03:38.700
further, and intervene if the new life is
headed for catastrophe. Then, there will

00:03:38.700 --> 00:03:42.480
most likely be a portion of society that
thinks we should physically visit Europa

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:46.260
and try to guide the life-forms to
success — a dangerous proposition, as

00:03:46.260 --> 00:03:49.740
humans would carry microbes with them
that could endanger the very life we're

00:03:49.740 --> 00:03:54.210
trying to protect. As one final
possibility, what if we were to discover

00:03:54.210 --> 00:03:58.640
the presence of a very primitive species
just starting to use simple tools? This

00:03:58.640 --> 00:04:02.840
would be the truest test of humanity's
disposition towards extraterrestrial life.

00:04:02.840 --> 00:04:06.630
As with the previous scenario we
could look to the zoo hypothesis for one

00:04:06.630 --> 00:04:10.890
example of how best to handle fledgling
life-forms. The zoo hypothesis suggests

00:04:10.890 --> 00:04:14.280
that intelligent civilizations would
refrain from visiting planets with life,

00:04:14.280 --> 00:04:18.090
to allow natural evolution and
socio-cultural development. It certainly

00:04:18.090 --> 00:04:21.660
seems like the most respectful way to
handle the situation, and it's not too

00:04:21.660 --> 00:04:24.810
much of a stretch of the imagination to
think that most world leaders could

00:04:24.810 --> 00:04:28.380
agree to contact these extraterrestrials
only once they had reached a certain

00:04:28.380 --> 00:04:32.330
technological or societal threshold. Of course, there would be others who

00:04:32.330 --> 00:04:36.200
would want to visit them immediately, and,
I don't know, build pyramids or something...

00:04:36.200 --> 00:04:40.250
While this may not seem like an
important topic to discuss, it allows us

00:04:40.250 --> 00:04:44.390
to explore several interesting issues.
How would our space programs change if

00:04:44.390 --> 00:04:47.900
we were the only life in the universe?
Would we scrap them entirely and just

00:04:47.900 --> 00:04:52.190
focus on our existence here on earth, or
would we reallocate those funds to focus

00:04:52.190 --> 00:04:56.120
on something more like an escape plan?
Would a newfound certainty of uniqueness

00:04:56.120 --> 00:04:59.870
in the universe prompt us to become a
more peaceful species, or would life go

00:04:59.870 --> 00:05:03.620
on the way it always has? If we
discovered we were not completely alone,

00:05:03.620 --> 00:05:07.040
but at the top of the totem pole of
intelligence, would we act like the

00:05:07.040 --> 00:05:10.970
curators of an intergalactic zoo? Would
we play the role of a benevolent god

00:05:10.970 --> 00:05:14.990
race, intervening to prevent disaster,
guiding life towards civilization? Or

00:05:14.990 --> 00:05:19.190
would we do the unthinkable, and ensure
that no life-forms ever become advanced

00:05:19.190 --> 00:05:23.360
enough to threaten our position as the
apex predator of the cosmos? These are

00:05:23.360 --> 00:05:27.200
only a handful of the questions this
topic can raise, and who knows if we'll

00:05:27.200 --> 00:05:30.050
ever be able to answer them? Only time
will tell.

